/target
/target-tts
**/*.rs.bk
# benchmarking scratch: corpora audio is fetched, never committed (the
# hash-pinned manifest in corpora/*.toml is the committed artifact)
/corpora/clips/
/.venv-bench/
# NOTE: bench/ledger.jsonl is deliberately NOT ignored. "Every claim traces
# to a ledger line" only holds if the ledger ships with the repo.
# model weights are data, not code — they live in the cache, never the repo
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.onnx
*.bin
# ...but the ORACLE FIXTURES are code's evidence, not weights. `*.bin` above
# exists to keep 77-488 MB checkpoints out; it also silently caught these,
# so three Carmenta oracle tests could only pass on a machine that happened
# to have them and failed on every fresh clone and in CI. For a project that
# gates everything on oracles, an oracle that cannot run is worse than one
# that fails. 1.2 MB total, and the exception is scoped to this one directory
# so a stray checkpoint still cannot ride in.
!/corpora/refs/fixtures/*.bin
# The same reasoning for the one fixture the `.bin` exception did not reach:
# `mobiledet_oracle_prob.safetensors` (0.3 MB) backs
# `mobiledet_probability_map_matches_paddle`. It was COMMITTED anyway, which
# left it tracked-and-ignored — and that is a state release-plz refuses to
# compute versions in ("the working directory of this project has uncommitted
# changes"), so every release run since the workflow landed failed before it
# could open a release PR. Nothing reached crates.io as a result.
#
# ONE FILE, not `*.safetensors`, and that distinction is load-bearing: this
# directory also holds the routing stage's real weights — formulanet at
# **564 MB**, slanet_plus at 7.3 MB, doclayout_s at 4.6 MB — which the blanket
# rule above exists to keep out. A glob exception here would leave 576 MB of
# checkpoints one `git add -A` away from the history. The `.bin` exception can
# be a glob because no weights in this directory carry that extension.
!/corpora/refs/fixtures/mobiledet_oracle_prob.safetensors
/.whispercpp/
/.tools-bench/
/corpora/fonts/
/.venv-diana/
# Detection bench cache: the YOLO26 checkpoints and the COCO annotation zip.
# The checkpoints are AGPL-3.0 and this repo is permissive — Diana never
# vendors or redistributes them (docs/diana-mission-plan.md §7), and `*.bin`
# above does not cover `.pt`, so without this line a `git add -A` would
# commit them. Everything here is fetched by tools/diana_coco_corpus.py and
# tools/diana_export_onnx.py.
/corpora/cache/
# ...and the same checkpoints when a tool drops one in the CWD instead. The
# rule above is path-scoped, so an `ultralytics` call run from the repo root
# leaves `yolo26n.pt` uncovered — 5.3 MB of AGPL-3.0 weights one `git add -A`
# away from being vendored into a permissive repo. This is the second instance
# of exactly what the note above protects against.
/yolo26*.pt
# TTS bench artifacts: generated audio is reproducible from the ledger line
# (voice hash + argv + corpus); the piper voice cache is fetched, never committed
/bench/tts-out/
/bench/mt1-substitution/
# Seed-spread sweep output: 5 seeds x a full run each, 91 MB, and entirely
# reproducible from the ledger line. Same rule as the two above.
/bench/seed-spread/
/.piper-voices/

# Mobile-det intermediates: regenerable by tools/carmenta_mobiledet_oracle.py
# and _ref.py, and large (12 MB of float activations). The fixtures the Rust
# oracle actually reads — the crop, its probability map, the reference boxes,
# and the measured-constant JSONs — ARE tracked.
corpora/refs/fixtures/mobiledet_probmap.npz
corpora/refs/fixtures/mobiledet_stages.npz

# Diana oracle activations: float tensors, regenerable by
# tools/diana_oracle_dump.py from the checkpoint plus a formula. The TRACKED
# artifacts are yolo26*_arch.json (the architecture read off the checkpoint)
# and yolo26*_oracle_digest.json (per-tensor shape/stats + 256 exact values
# at fixed indices) — together they let the Rust oracle run a real gate on a
# fresh clone, which is the standard the Carmenta fixture incident set,
# while the full-tensor max-abs comparison upgrades automatically when the
# npz files are present.
#
# GLOB, not two file names. This was two literal `yolo26n_...` lines while
# the port was n-only; the moment m/l/x were oracled it silently stopped
# covering anything, and `git add -A` would have staged 700 MB of dumps.
# An ignore rule enumerated per instance is a rule that expires the first
# time the thing it protects against gets a second instance.
corpora/refs/fixtures/yolo26*_oracle_*.npz
tools/__pycache__/
corpora/refs/__pycache__/
# Scratch target dir for -C target-cpu=native experiments (docs/whys/diana-latency.md
# D6f). Kept separate so the normal build is not invalidated by the flag.
/target-native

# scratch build dir used to compile while a measurement holds target/
target-gate/

# wasm-bindgen output: a build artifact, regenerated by the command in the README
crates/ffai-wasm/pkg/
crates/ffai-wasm/pkg-node/
# `pkg-dl` / `pkg-ra` are two more build arms, same as Carmenta's below.
crates/ffai-wasm/pkg-dl/
crates/ffai-wasm/pkg-ra/
# Same for Carmenta's module. `pkg-simd` / `pkg-candle` are the two arms of the
# conv A/B — wasm cannot switch at run time, so the arms are two builds.
crates/ffai-carmenta-wasm/pkg/
crates/ffai-carmenta-wasm/pkg-node/
crates/ffai-carmenta-wasm/pkg-simd/
crates/ffai-carmenta-wasm/pkg-candle/

# The Great Gate rule-search tool — LOCAL ONLY, NEVER PUBLISHED.
# Imported 2026-08-08 from remade_ffmpeg_rs@ae6b5ce (_greatgate/, itself
# gitignored there). It is a detached cargo workspace with publish = false and
# sits outside `members = ["crates/*"]`, so it cannot join the FFai crate tree
# or be picked up by `cargo publish --workspace`. This ignore is the third,
# belt-and-braces guard: offline analysis tooling and its harvests never ship.
/_greatgate/
/.venv-argus/
# VLM prediction dumps: kept ON DISK deliberately (re-scoring must be free —
# see crates/ffai-bench/src/vlm.rs), but they are regenerable model output, not
# evidence. The ledger line is the committed artifact, exactly as with
# /corpora/clips/.
/bench/predictions/
# Step-3 vision-tower oracle dumps: full tensors, megabytes, regenerable by
# `corpora/refs/dump_smolvlm_vision.py`. The committed artifact is the small
# summary.json the dumper also writes.
/.oracle/

# Claude Code's per-machine permission allowlist. Its entries embed absolute
# local paths, which are machine-specific noise in a public repo rather
# than project source.
.claude/
